    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#di-xin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/di-xin.jpg" alt="Di Xin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#di-xin">Di Xin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%B5%B5%E6%9C%AC%E4%B8%89%E5%9B%BD%E5%A6%96%E5%A9%A6%E4%BC%9D_%E6%AE%B7%E7%B4%82%E7%8E%8B.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">高井 伴寛、蹄斎 北馬 (Hiroshi Takai, Hossai Hokuma), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zhou were a people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wei-river">Wei River</a> valley in modern Shaanxi. According to their own traditions, their ancestor Danfu, the Ancient Duke, led the clan to the plain of Zhouyuan below Mount Qi, where archaeologists have found large building foundations and inscribed oracle bones. Under King Wen, who held the title Lord of the West as a Shang ally, the Zhou extended their influence over neighbouring polities and moved their seat to Feng on the Feng River. His son King Wu led the Zhou and a coalition of allied peoples east and defeated the last Shang king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#di-xin">Di Xin</a>, at the Battle of Muye, a date given as 1046 BCE by the Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project, although scholars propose dates between about 1050 and 1027 BCE. The <em>Li gui</em>, a bronze vessel cast days after the battle, confirms that the conquest took place on the morning of a <em>jiazi</em> day. Wu died a few years later, and his brother the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a> became regent for the young King Cheng. The duke suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rebellion-of-the-three-guards">Rebellion of the Three Guards</a>, in which the Shang prince Wu Geng joined Wu's brothers Guanshu and Caishu, campaigned in the east for three years and built a second capital, Chengzhou, at Luoyi near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#luoyang">Luoyang</a>. The main royal capital remained Haojing, founded by King Wu beside Feng near modern Xi'an.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#classic-of-poetry"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry.jpg" alt="Classic of Poetry" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%9C%A8%E9%90%B8_---_Confucian_wooden-clapper_bell.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aethelwolf Emsworth., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Western Zhou society was organised around lineages. The royal Ji clan and allied clans formed an aristocracy whose status was expressed in ancestral temples, sacrifices and sets of bronze vessels whose number and type marked rank; a ritual reform in the ninth century BCE standardised these sets and changed the music of the bells used in ceremonies. Below the aristocracy lived farmers who worked land on behalf of lineage heads; the later account by Mencius of the well-field system, with eight families farming a shared ninth plot, is probably an idealisation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet">Millet</a> was the staple, together with wheat and soybeans, and many of the poems in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#classic-of-poetry">Book of Songs</a></em> describe the agricultural year, feasting and the duties of nobles. Wealth took the form of land, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cowrie-shell">cowries</a>, bronze, horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chariot">chariots</a> and textiles; inscriptions record land exchanges, compensation in disputes and royal gifts of cowries, metal, garments and banners. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bronze">Bronze</a>, jade and pottery production was concentrated in workshops near the capitals, such as the large casting site at Zhouyuan. Religion continued Shang ancestor worship but gave a central place to Heaven (<em>Tian</em>), and the kings called themselves Sons of Heaven. Divination with yarrow stalks, later systematised in the <em>Book of Changes</em>, joined oracle-bone divination. The Zhou took over Shang scribes and artisans, which explains the continuity of writing and bronze techniques.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#duke-of-zhou"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou.jpg" alt="Duke of Zhou" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duke_of_Zhou.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Western Zhou kings maintained two large standing forces mentioned in bronze inscriptions: the Six Armies of the West, stationed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wei-river">Wei River</a> valley, and the Eight Armies of Chengzhou in the east, which may have included former Shang troops. Armies combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chariot">chariots</a> with infantry armed with dagger-axes, spears and bows. Expansion was strongest in the first century of the dynasty. King Wu and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a> conquered the Shang heartland, and King Kang and King Zhao campaigned east into Shandong and south towards the Huai and Han rivers; the <em>Xiao Yu ding</em> inscription records a victory over the Guifang with thousands of captives. King Zhao's southern expedition against Chu, around 957 BCE, ended in disaster: the king died near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#han-river">Han River</a>, and later texts say the Six Armies were lost. Thereafter the Zhou were mostly on the defensive. The Huaiyi of the south-east and the Xianyun of the north-west became persistent enemies in the ninth century BCE. King Xuan (r. 827–782 BCE) restored Zhou military prestige with campaigns against the Xianyun, recorded on vessels such as the <em>Duoyou ding</em> and the <em>Guoji Zi Bai pan</em>, and against the Huaiyi. The regional lords played a growing role in defence, and their armies, especially those of the State of Jin, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> and the State of Zheng, eventually outweighed the king's.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Western Zhou government rested on kinship. Instead of ruling the conquered lands directly, the early kings established regional states held by royal relatives and allies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a>'s son founded the State of Lu, King Cheng's brother the State of Jin, the Duke of Shao's family the State of Yan, and Jiang Ziya the State of Qi; the philosopher Xunzi later wrote that the Duke of Zhou set up 71 states, 53 of them held by members of the Ji clan. Regional rulers owed military service, attendance at court and ritual tribute, and investiture inscriptions on bronzes such as the <em>Yihou Ze gui</em> record grants of land and people. At the centre the kings developed an increasingly bureaucratic administration, with officials such as the Minister of Land (<em>situ</em>), the Minister of War (<em>sima</em>) and the Minister of Works (<em>sikong</em>), and formal appointment ceremonies recorded in hundreds of inscriptions, the longest being the nearly 500-character <em>Mao Gong ding</em>. The doctrine of the Mandate of Heaven (<em>tianming</em>), set out in chapters of the <em>Book of Documents</em> attributed to the Duke of Zhou, held that Heaven entrusted rule to the virtuous and withdrew it from the wicked. In 841 BCE a revolt in the capital drove out King Li, and a regency known as the Gonghe governed until 828 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#warring-states-period"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warring-states-period.jpg" alt="Warring States period" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#warring-states-period">Warring States period</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EN-WarringStatesAll260BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philg88, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Western Zhou left the most durable political ideas of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>. The Mandate of Heaven became the standard justification of dynastic change for three thousand years, invoked by founders of dynasties from the Han to the Qing, and Son of Heaven remained the title of Chinese emperors until 1912. The regional states created by the Zhou grew into the great powers of the Spring and Autumn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#warring-states-period">Warring States periods</a>, and the ritual and kinship culture they shared defined membership of the Zhou world, the root of later ideas of a common Chinese civilisation. The <em>He zun</em>, a bronze cast under King Cheng, contains the earliest known use of the phrase <em>zhongguo</em>, 'the central state', later the Chinese name for China. Confucius regarded the early Western Zhou, and above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a>, as the model of good government and ritual order, so that later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#confucianism">Confucianism</a> looked back on it as a golden age. The oldest layers of the classic texts, including the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#classic-of-poetry">Book of Songs</a></em>, parts of the <em>Book of Documents</em> and the core of the <em>Book of Changes</em>, are traditionally dated to this period. Thousands of inscribed bronzes, many from hoards buried when noble families fled east around 771 BCE, are a major source for the history of Chinese writing, law and ritual.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#luoyang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luoyang.jpg" alt="Luoyang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#luoyang">Luoyang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Longmen_Grottoes_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rialfver Original uploader was Rialfver at nl.wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Royal power weakened steadily from the tenth century BCE as the kings granted away land to reward officials and allies, while regional lords became hereditary dynasties with their own interests. The expulsion of King Li in 841 BCE showed the fragility of the court, and the vigorous reign of King Xuan was only a partial recovery. Under King You (r. 781–771 BCE) the court split over the succession. You favoured his consort Bao Si and her son Bofu, and deposed Queen Shen and the crown prince Yijiu, who fled to his maternal grandfather, the Marquis of Shen. In 771 BCE the Marquis of Shen, together with the State of Zeng and the Quanrong attacked the capital, killed King You at the foot of Mount Li and sacked Haojing. The story in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a></em> that You had lost the trust of his lords by lighting the warning beacons to amuse Bao Si is a later legend. Yijiu was enthroned as King Ping and, with the support of the lords of Jin, Zheng and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, moved the court to Chengzhou near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#luoyang">Luoyang</a>, traditionally in 770 BCE, though the bamboo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscript</a> <em>Xinian</em> suggests a later date. A rival king, Yuchen, was backed by other lords for some years. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wei-river">Wei River</a> valley was left to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#state-of-qin">State of Qin</a>, and the Zhou kings never regained real power.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Western Zhou overthrew the Shang and created the political vocabulary of imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>: the Mandate of Heaven, the king as Son of Heaven and rule through kinsmen in regional states, which later grew into the rival powers of the Spring and Autumn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#warring-states-period">Warring States periods</a>.</em></p>

